cite26†Nadia Labai , cite27†Joseph Lilien , cite28†Divya Raghunathan , cite29†Zvonimir Rakamarić , cite30†Niloofar Razavi , cite31†Michael Tautschnig , cite32†Ali Torkamani , cite33†Nathaniel Weir , cite34†Michael W. Whalen , cite35†Jianan Yao L12: View a PDF of the paper titled A Neurosymbolic Approach to Natural Language Formalization and Verification, by Chenyang An and 29 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite36†View PDF cite37†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Large Language Models perform well at natural language interpretation and reasoning, but their lack of formal correctness guarantees limits their adoption in regulated industries like finance and health-care that operate under strict policies.\nL16: To address this limitation, we launched Automated Reasoning checks (ARc): a public service that (1) uses LLMs with optional human guidance to formalize natural language policies, allowing fine-grained control of the formalization process, and (2) uses inference-time autoformalization to validate logical correctness of natural language statements against those policies. ARc performs multiple redundant formalization steps at inference time, checking the formalizations for semantic equivalence.\nL17: Our benchmarks show that ARc exceeds 99% soundness and achieves a near-zero false positive rate in identifying logical validity. Our approach produces auditable artifacts that substantiate the verification outcomes and can be used to improve the original text. ARc is the first commercial offering from a major cloud provider to integrate automated reasoning into a generative AI guardrail.\nL18: Comments:  | 28 pages, 11 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite38†arXiv:2511.09008 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite39†arXiv:2511.09008v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite40†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09008†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Sam Bayless [cite41†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 06:00:37 UTC (615 KB)\nL27: [v2] Mon, 13 Jul 2026 19:54:42 UTC (514 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled A Neurosymbolic Approach to Natural Language Formalization and Verification, by Chenyang An and 29 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite36†View PDF L36:   * cite37†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite42†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite43†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite44†< prev |   cite45†next > L45: \nL46: cite46†new | cite47†recent | cite48†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite49†cs L51: cite50†cs.AI L52: cite51†cs.LG L53: cite52†cs.LO L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite53†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite54†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite55†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09197] The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09197)\nciteturn5976view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09197\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 19 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Francois Meyer , cite7†Jan Buys L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages, by Francois Meyer and Jan Buys\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Subword segmentation is typically applied in preprocessing and stays fixed during training. Alternatively, it can be learned during training to optimise the training objective.\nL17: In this paper we study the learning dynamics of subword segmentation: if a language model can dynamically optimise tokenisation, how do its subwords evolve during pretraining and finetuning? To explore this, we extend the subword segmental language model (SSLM), a framework for learning subwords during training, to support pretraining and finetuning.\nL18: We train models for three typologically diverse languages to study learning dynamics across the morphological spectrum: Isi-Xhosa is conjunctive (long word forms composed of many morphemes), Setswana is disjunctive (morphemes written as separate words), and English represents a typological middle ground. We analyse subword dynamics from a linguistic perspective, tracking morphology, productivity, and fertility.\nL19: We identify four stages of subword learning, with the morphologically complex isi-Xhosa exhibiting greater instability. During finetuning, subword boundaries shift to become finer-grained. Lastly, we show that learnable subwords offers a promising approach to improve text generation and cross-lingual transfer for low-resource, morphologically complex languages.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.09197 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.09197v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09197†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Francois Meyer [cite13†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 10:52:33 UTC (10,172 KB)\nL28: [v2] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 09:30:00 UTC (1,454 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages, by Francois Meyer and Jan Buys\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†View PDF L37:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L46: \nL47: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite21†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite25†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite26†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite27†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite28†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Code, Data, Media\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09232] POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Speech-to-Text Translation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09232)\nciteturn5976view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09232\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 31 Mar 2026 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Speech-to-Text Translation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xuanchen Li , cite7†Chenrui Cui , cite8†Tianrui Wang , cite9†Meng Ge , cite10†Zikang Huang , cite11†Yizhou Peng , cite12†Jin Li , cite13†Yuheng Lu , cite14†Yu Jiang , cite15†Nyima Tashi , cite16†Longbiao Wang , cite17†Jianwu Dang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Speech-to-Text Translation, by Xuanchen Li and 11 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite18†View PDF cite19†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Speech Large Language Models have achieved breakthroughs in multilingual speech-to-text translation. However, existing approaches often overlook semantic commonalities across source languages, leading to biased translation performance. In this work, we propose POTSA (Parallel Optimal Transport for Speech Alignment), a new framework based on cross-lingual parallel speech pairs and Optimal Transport, designed to bridge high- and low-resource translation gaps.\nL17: First, we introduce a Bias Compensation module to coarsely align initial speech representations. Second, we impose token-level OT constraints on a Q-Former using parallel pairs to establish fine-grained representation consistency. Then, we apply a layer scheduling strategy to focus OT constraints on semantically beneficial layers.\nL18: Experiments on FLEURS show our method achieves SOTA performance, with +1.29 BLEU over five common languages and +2.93 BLEU on zero-shot languages, using only 10 hours of parallel speech per language.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Sound (cs.SD)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite20†arXiv:2511.09232 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite21†arXiv:2511.09232v3 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite22†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09232†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Xuanchen Li [cite23†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 11:47:56 UTC (509 KB)\nL27: cite24†[v2] Mon, 30 Mar 2026 07:44:23 UTC (520 KB)\nL28: [v3] Tue, 31 Mar 2026 01:39:46 UTC (520 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Speech-to-Text Translation, by Xuanchen Li and 11 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite18†View PDF L37:   * cite19†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite25†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite26†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite27†< prev |   cite28†next > L46: \nL47: cite29†new | cite30†recent | cite31†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite32†cs L52: cite33†cs.SD L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite34†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite35†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite36†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite37†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite38†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite39†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL86: \nL87: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL88: \nL89: scite Smart Citations (cite40†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL90: \nL91: [Input] Code, Data, Media\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09292] C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09292)\nciteturn5976view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09292\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 16 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yu Li , cite7†Zhe Yang , cite8†Yi Huang , cite9†Xin Liu , cite10†Guilin Qi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation, by Yu Li and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable text generation capabilities. However, controlling specific attributes of generated text remains challenging without architectural modifications or extensive fine-tuning. Current methods typically toggle a single, basic attribute but struggle with precise multi-attribute control.\nL17: In scenarios where attribute requirements conflict, existing methods lack coordination mechanisms, causing interference between desired attributes. Furthermore, these methods fail to incorporate iterative optimization processes in the controlled generation pipeline. To address these limitations, we propose Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation (C$^3$TG), a two-phase framework for fine-grained, multi-dimensional text attribute control.\nL18: During generation, C$^3$TG selectively pairs the LLM with the required attribute classifiers from the 17 available dimensions and employs weighted KL-divergence to adjust token probabilities. The optimization phase then leverages an energy function combining classifier scores and penalty terms to resolve attribute conflicts through iterative feedback, enabling precise control over multiple dimensions simultaneously while preserving natural text flow.\nL19: Experiments show that C$^3$TG significantly outperforms baselines across multiple metrics including attribute accuracy, linguistic fluency, and output diversity, while simultaneously reducing toxicity. These results establish C$^3$TG as an effective and flexible solution for multi-dimensional text attribute control that requires no costly model modifications.\nL20: Comments:  | This paper has been accepted as a poster presentation at AAAI-2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.09292 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.09292v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09292†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Yu Li [cite16†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 12:59:23 UTC (2,142 KB)\nL29: [v2] Sun, 16 Nov 2025 14:19:40 UTC (2,142 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation, by Yu Li and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite11†View PDF L38:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite17†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite18†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: cite25†cs.AI L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.08873] UCO: A Multi-Turn Interactive Reinforcement Learning Method for Adaptive Teaching with Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08873)\nciteturn5976view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08873\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 26 Aug 2026 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:UCO: A Multi-Turn Interactive Reinforcement Learning Method for Adaptive Teaching with Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Shouang Wei , cite7†Min Zhang , cite8†Xin Lin , cite9†Bo Jiang , cite10†Kun Kuang , cite11†Jingyuan Chen , cite12†Zhongxiang Dai L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled UCO: A Multi-Turn Interactive Reinforcement Learning Method for Adaptive Teaching with Large Language Models, by Shouang Wei and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) are shifting from answer providers to intelligent tutors in educational settings, yet current supervised fine-tuning methods only learn surface teaching patterns without dynamic adaptation capabilities. Recent reinforcement learning approaches address this limitation but face two critical challenges.\nL17: First, they evaluate teaching effectiveness solely based on whether students produce correct outputs, unable to distinguish whether students genuinely understand or echo teacher-provided answers during interaction. Second, they cannot perceive students' evolving cognitive states in real time through interactive dialogue, thus failing to adapt teaching strategies to match students' cognitive levels dynamically. We propose the Unidirectional Cognitive Optimization (UCO) method to address these challenges.\nL18: UCO uses a multi-turn interactive reinforcement learning paradigm where the innovation lies in two synergistic reward functions: the Progress Reward captures students' cognitive advancement, evaluating whether students truly transition from confusion to comprehension, while the Scaffold Reward dynamically identifies each student's Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD), encouraging teachers to maintain productive teaching within this zone.\nL19: We evaluate UCO by comparing it against 11 baseline models on BigMath and MathTutorBench benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that our UCO model outperforms all models of equivalent scale and achieves performance comparable to advanced closed-source models. The code and data are available at cite15†this https URL†github.com .\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted to EMNLP 2026 (Main Conference)\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2511.08873 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2511.08873v4 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08873†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Shouang Wei [cite19†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 01:27:02 UTC (1,230 KB)\nL29: cite20†[v2] Mon, 5 Jan 2026 15:26:03 UTC (1,231 KB)\nL30: cite21†[v3] Tue, 25 Aug 2026 15:46:25 UTC (1,383 KB)\nL31: [v4] Wed, 26 Aug 2026 05:52:41 UTC (1,376 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled UCO: A Multi-Turn Interactive Reinforcement Learning Method for Adaptive Teaching with Large Language Models, by Shouang Wei and 6 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite13†View PDF L39:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite22†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite23†view license L43: \nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.AI\nL47: \nL48: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L49: \nL50: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2025-11 L51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: cite29†cs L55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \n"
